  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe low that has been plaguing the region the past few days, is\nfinally moving across Idaho today. We will continue to see\npockets of showers and storms through this evening. Rainfall\namounts should not be terribly high, put we certainly could see\nsome locally heavier rainfall rates and small hail in this\nsituation. Overnight, the low continues to shift east of the\nDivide with some lingering showers around Island Park and\nDriggs. With cooler air filtering in, we will likely see some\npockets of frost across the Snake Plain and Magic Valley as lows\ndrop toward the mid 30s in more rural spots. Colder valley\nlocations in the mountains will likely see frost or a light\nfreeze. This potential also exists tomorrow night in the same\nlocations. Otherwise we are dry until Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\nThe next closed low offshore shifts inland across California,\nthen north across Utah and Idaho later Tuesday and Wednesday.\nRight now, the Blend of Models doesn't have a ton of moisture or\na higher chance of showers and storms with the forecast. That\nseems to make sense based on a lack of significant tropical\nsource from the Pacific, or at least one that will reach\nIdaho.We will need to see if that trend persists. We look dry\nagain until next weekend, when the next storm develops offshore\nand shifts into the western U.S. Temperatures quickly jump 5-15\ndegrees on Sunday, but still not out of the 60s and 70s. After\nthat, the warming trend continues but is much slower on a daily\nbasis. By mid to late week, we will see a few more 80s for\nafternoon highs for our lower valleys.",
